    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d285">

                <pb facs="0440" n="322" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.9318/129</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Secretary of
                    War</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-11-20"><hi rendition="#italic">November 20,
                        1928</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to your letter of
                    September 6, 1928,<anchor n="95" xml:id="d285-f1"/> requesting to be informed
                    whether the troops and agencies of the United States Army in China have been
                    satisfactory to this Department from the point of view of the American Minister
                    to China, and the Department’s reply of September 20, 1928,<anchor n="96"
                        xml:id="d285-f2"/> stating that the American Minister had been instructed to
                    prepare a report on this subject. I now have the honor to transmit herewith a
                    copy of the latter’s confidential despatch No. 1679 dated September 27, 1928, in
                    which he sets forth his appreciation of the cooperation rendered him by the
                    forces of the United States Army in China. I may add that this Department shares
                    entirely the views expressed by Mr. MacMurray, and I trust that you may see fit
                    to communicate to the Commander of those forces the substance of the Minister’s
                    statement <pb facs="0441" n="323" xml:id="pg323"/> of appreciation in the manner
                    indicated in the final paragraph of his despatch.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank B. Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d285-f1" n="95" xml:id="d285-f1-content">
                <p>Brig. Gen. Joseph C. Castner, commanding U. S. Army forces in China, May 15,
                    1926, to March 17, 1929, when the status of this force as a separate command was
                    terminated.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d285-f2" n="96" xml:id="d285-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
